From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: translate a subtitle with a language model the viewer chooses

Many videos carry subtitles, just not in the language the viewer reads:
an English film with English and German tracks for a French viewer. With
"Translate subtitles" on, Kodi offers such a video one more subtitle,
"Auto-translated to French (from English)", after the video's own, and
plays it the way it plays a subtitle in the viewer's language. The
language is the subtitle language setting; a video that has a subtitle
in it is left as it is, and the viewer's choice for a video still wins.

The track translated is the first, in the order Kodi chooses subtitles,
that is text - SubRip or plain text inside the video, or a SubRip file
beside it - in a declared language and not forced. A forced track is
never translated and does not count as a subtitle in the viewer's
language: it only covers what is said in another language, so an English
film with French forced lines still gets its English translated.

The translation is a language model's, through the OpenAI chat API that
OpenRouter, OpenAI and the servers people run themselves (llama.cpp,
vLLM, Ollama) all speak. Small translation models drop clauses and read
idioms word for word; a general model of a few billion parameters does
neither, and a set-top box cannot run one. Player > Subtitles gains the
provider, its address for a server of one's own, the key, and the model,
typed or chosen from the list the provider gives, with a button that
translates one line to test them. The cues go a few to a request, with
the lines before them, the title and the characters' names, and the
model answers with the same numbered lines; an answer that loses the
numbering is asked again line by line, and a line far longer than its
source is refused, and so is an answer with a remark after its lines or
one far too long to be an answer. A reasoning model is asked not to
reason first. A provider that does not answer, or does not list the
model, offers no translation: it is asked in the background when its
settings change and every ten minutes - half a minute after it did not
answer - and the first time a video needs it, which waits for the answer
a second and a half at most.

A cue goes to the model as it shows: a "<...>" or "{...}" closed on its
line is left out, since Kodi and libass hide it, and a "<" or "{" that
closes nothing stays, since it shows - some tracks open a voice off
screen with "< ".

Selecting the entry opens its track with the language to translate into.
The player no longer reopens a subtitle already selected, and the entry
is its source track read in another language, so the one condition the
player gains is that a subtitle is reopened when the language it would
be read in is not the one it was opened in: picking the entry with its
source showing, or the source with the entry showing, reopens it, and
picking the subtitle showing does not.
The entry is CTranslatedEntry's to offer, select and describe; the
player only calls it, and the cue stage the repair added carries the
translation's thread for the decoder and the subtitle file alike.
Inside the video, the text decoder hands each repaired cue to a thread
of its own and the cue reaches libass when its translation is ready, the
way it would have without: a track inside the video is read seconds
before its cues are shown, and a request takes a second or a few, so the
player never waits. A seek drops whatever was not yet delivered and ends
the request for it; a cue the provider fails still ends the one left
open before it. Once playing, the viewer's choice of subtitle stands
when the streams change. A file beside the video is known whole,
so it is translated from where playback is, ahead of it. A dialogue cue
keeps one speaker a line; any other is translated as one sentence and
laid out on two lines. A line in capitals - a song, a sign - goes in
sentence case, since models read capitals as names, and comes back in
capitals; the repair's letter case serves, Greek and Cyrillic included.
Closing the track ends the request in flight.

A subtitle file now carries the language its name gives into its hints,
as a track inside the video does.

Image subtitles cannot be translated.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
